.seg button.on{background:var(--accent);color:#fff}
.bar{display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center}
code{background:var(--bg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 10px;display:block;overflow-x:auto;white-space:nowrap;font-size:12px}
.note{color:var(--mut);font-size:12px;margin-top:10px}
.st{font-size:12px;color:var(--mut);min-height:1.4em}
select,input[type=text]{font:inherit;background:var(--card);color:var(--fg);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:7px;padding:6px}
</style>
<div class=wrap>
<h1>DP-6 Color</h1>
<p class=sub>Writes go straight to the panel over DDC/CI. The values here are the source of truth &mdash; the monitor's gain register is write-only and always reports 49&#8202;/&#8202;46&#8202;/&#8202;50 no matter what's loaded.</p>

<div class="card warn">
<b>One gain channel at a time.</b> This panel has a single gain register plus a channel selector, not three independent gains. Switching channel drops the previous one back to its default &mdash; so you can correct a cast in one direction, but you can't balance two channels against each other.
</div>

<div class=card>
  <div class=row><label>Channel</label>
